"question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x) = x^2$ on $\\mathbb{R}$, why does fail injectivity?","options":["Different inputs can have the same output","Every input has no output","The function is not defined on $\\mathbb{R}$","The function is not continuous on $\\mathbb{R}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1605e25532e38d2eafad5a14"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_injective_function_4ddbd6da_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How can restricting the domain change whether a formula is injective?","options":["Changing the domain never affects injectivity","The domain can affect injectivity.","Every polynomial is injective on every domain","A function is injective only if it is constant"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"badbc5f8d6184f7c4834efc2"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_injective_function_4ddbd6da_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is a strictly increasing function automatically injective?","options":["Because $x<y$ forces $f(x)<f(y)$.","Because it must be continuous","Because its range is all of $\\mathbb{R}$","Because it is bounded"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"128ce3816069d098d6c63f4c"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_injective_function_4ddbd6da_011","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Is a constant function $f(x)=c$ injective on a domain with at least two points?","options":["No; many inputs share the output $c$.","Yes; constants are always injective","Only if $c=0$","Only on infinite domains"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"78da40aa469f1e07985d4443"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_injective_function_4ddbd6da_012","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Injectivity is a statement about which of these?","options":["Whether two different inputs can share an output","Whether every codomain element is attained","Whether the function is continuous","Whether the domain equals the codomain"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f600daffffcb02792b78462e"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_injective_function_4ddbd6da_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Graphically, $f$ is injective when every horizontal line $y=c$ meets the graph:","options":["at most once","at least once","exactly twice","never"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"95dffb7536c3b9ad8d3cd3dd"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_injective_function_4ddbd6da_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which implication is equivalent to injectivity when written contrapositively?","options":["$x=y\\implies f(x)\\neq f(y)$","$f(x)=f(y)\\implies x=y$","$f(x)\\neq f(y)\\implies x=y$","$x\\neq y\\implies f(x)=f(y)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f5f559d8e7860f211d068939"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_injective_function_4ddbd6da_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $f$ is injective and you know the value $f(x)$, what can you recover about $x$?","options":["The input $x$ is determined uniquely","Nothing at all about $x$","Two possible values of $x$","Only the sign of $x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4a33b7f5dba8f348d9a63b79"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_injective_function_4ddbd6da_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x)=x^2$ on $\\mathbb R$, which pair shows non-injectivity?","options":["$1$ and $-1$","$1$ and $2$","$0$ and $1$","$2$ and $3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8316e76af83c03bd11f8f62a"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_injective_function_4ddbd6da_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $f$ is injective and $f(a)=f(b)$, what follows?","options":["$a\\neq b$","$a=b$","$f(a)=0$","$f(b)=1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e9398d254555a8fc8f1b5262"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_injective_function_4ddbd6da_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $f$ and $g$ are injective and $g\\circ f$ is defined, is $g\\circ f$ injective?","options":["Yes","No, never","Only if $f=g$","Only if both are also surjective"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"03a0009ca30627bed8df2e0b"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_injective_function_4ddbd6da_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which restriction makes $f(x)=x^2$ injective?","options":["Domain $\\mathbb R$","Domain $[-1,1]$","Domain $[0,+\\infty)$","Domain $\\{-1,1\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0927ca867dfa4ecc4d26db3d"},{"id":"repair_injective_function_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $X=\\{1,2\\}$ and define $f(1)=a$, $f(2)=b$ with $a\\neq b$. Is $f$ injective?","options":["No, because $X$ has more than one element.","Yes, because every function from a finite set is injective.","No, because injectivity requires $a=b$.","Yes, because the two different inputs have different outputs."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"db50790f01d3e8b97b0b369e"}]}}